  "answer_text": "The iconic scene where Del Boy falls through the bar is from the episode **\"Yuppy Love\"**! Here are all the details:\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udcfa The Episode: *\"Yuppy Love\"*\n**\"Yuppy Love\"** was the first episode of Series 6 of *Only Fools and Horses*, airing on **8th January 1989** on BBC1, with a viewing figure of **13.9 million**.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83c\udf7a The Scene\nIn a wine bar, Del is leaning against a folding section at the end of the bar when he spots an attractive young woman looking at him. A barman comes along and lifts up the section Del was leaning against to get through, but doesn't put it back down \u2014 leaving a gap. This happens behind Del's back and he doesn't notice at all. Del then says *\"Play it nice and cool, son. Nice and cool, you know what I mean...\"* \u2014 and leans back, falling through the gap onto the floor.\n\nThe moment is made even more perfect by Trigger's shocked reaction, looking around for Del, who is firmly on the floor.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83c\udfad Behind the Scenes\n- The scene was inspired by writer **John Sullivan** watching a man do the exact same thing in a real wine bar, though the man grabbed onto the fixed part of the bar and didn't fall all the way over. Sullivan thought it funny for the man's body language, trying to recover his cool.\n\n- Sullivan originally wanted a slip, stumble, and a tree-like fall, but **David Jason** thought Del should go all the way over \u2014 starting to go sideways without looking in the direction of the fall, which Jason thought was the key to the scene. There was a hidden crash mat, but it was a hard shot to get because it was difficult not to look where Jason was falling.\n\n- A rehearsal script for the episode that went up for auction showed that **Trigger wasn't supposed to be in the scene originally**. However, Roger Lloyd Pack (who played Trigger) was there on the day, and John Sullivan decided he should be included after all.\n\n- Roger Lloyd Pack later revealed that Jason never performed the fall during rehearsals, so his reaction was genuine \u2014 admitting it was a surprise when it happened on the day.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83c\udfc6 Legacy\nDel Boy's fall through the bar has become one of the most celebrated sitcom scenes ever. Jason gets people asking him about that fall all the time, and some never like to talk about anything else, but he's happy to be remembered for something so iconic.",
